NAME
    colors - ANSI color code variable definitions for bash scripts

SYNOPSIS
    source colors
    . colors

DESCRIPTION
    The colors utility provides a collection of predefined environment variables containing ANSI escape sequences[cite: 1]. By sourcing this file into your bash scripts or current shell session, you can easily apply styling to your terminal output without needing to memorize raw escape codes.

    Note: This file is not meant to be executed directly. It must be included in your environment or scripts using the `source` or `.` command.

VARIABLE CONVENTIONS
    The variables are predictably named using a combination of position, style, and color[cite: 1].

    Positions:
        fg  - Foreground text color[cite: 1].
        bg  - Background color[cite: 1].

    Styles:
        (none) - Standard text[cite: 1].
        b      - Bold text (e.g., \e[1;31m)[cite: 1].
        u      - Underlined text (e.g., \e[4;32m)[cite: 1].

    Colors:
        Black, Red, Green, Yellow, Blue, Magenta, Cyan, White[cite: 1].

SPECIAL VARIABLES
    reset
        Resets all text formatting back to the terminal's default state (\e[0m)[cite: 1]. Always append this to the end of your styled strings to prevent color bleeding into the next terminal line.

EXAMPLES
    source colors
        Loads the color variables into your current shell or script so they can be referenced.

    echo -e "${fgGreen}Operation completed successfully.${reset}"
        Prints the message in standard green text, then resets the formatting[cite: 1]. Note that the `-e` flag is required for `echo` to interpret the escape sequences properly.

    echo -e "${fgbRed}ERROR:${reset} File not found."
        Prints "ERROR:" in bold red text, and the rest of the message in the terminal's default color[cite: 1].

    echo -e "${bguYellow}WARNING${reset}"
        Prints the word "WARNING" with an underlined yellow background[cite: 1].